    "chip-glyph": {
      "$type": "color",
      "$value": "oklch(100% 0 0)",
      "$description": "white in BOTH schemes — see why",
      "$extensions": {
        "silvicom.section": [
          "⚠ NOT `--ink-inverse`, which is the obvious reach and is wrong here: it flips to oklch(21.5% 0 0) in dark, where it would put a near-black glyph on a saturated chip. `--ink-inverse` means \"ink for an inverted SURFACE\", and a chip's ground is saturated in both schemes rather than inverted in one. The stops above are chosen so white clears 3:1 on every tone in both schemes, which is what makes one glyph colour the right answer."
        ],
        "silvicom.expose": "color"
      }
    },
    "chip-top-edge": {
      "$type": "color",
      "$value": "color-mix(in oklab, oklch(100% 0 0) 28%, transparent)",
      "$description": "light catching the top of a solid object; an ingredient, not a utility"
    },
    "chip-danger-from": {
      "$type": "color",
      "$value": "var(--ramp-danger-500)",
      "$extensions": {
        "silvicom.expose": "color",
        "silvicom.section": [
          "the solid icon chip (D-DT17). One hue's ramp, two stops, a white glyph and the chip's own colour cast on the card beneath — the `card 4` treatment, which concentrates a tile's colour into its 40px chip so the tile's ground can stay neutral and the four KPI figures stay comparable (that is D-DT1 pushed further, not reversed). ⚠ THE STOPS ARE NOT THE SAME STEPS IN BOTH SCHEMES, and they cannot be: the dark ramps turn over between 300 and 400 (dark `success-400` is L 79%, `success-300` is 48%), so the light pair 500→700 renders as a PALE chip in dark and the white glyph drops to 1.78:1 on it. Measured 2026-09-20 across all seven tones: light 500→700 holds the glyph at 3.27:1 worst (danger) and 3.73:1 best; dark 600→300 holds it at 3.29:1 worst (success) and 5.29:1 best. Both clear WCAG 1.4.11's 3:1 for a non-text graphic. Neutral is the one tone whose dark `from` is 500 rather than 600, because its dark ramp turns one step earlier — 600 (L 45.5%) is DARKER than 300 (L 49.9%), which would light the chip from below while every other tone lit it from above."
        ]
      }
    },
    "chip-danger-to": {
      "$type": "color",
      "$value": "var(--ramp-danger-700)",
      "$extensions": {
        "silvicom.expose": "color"
      }
    },
    "chip-danger-glow": {
      "$type": "color",
      "$value": "color-mix(in oklab, var(--chip-danger-to) 55%, transparent)",
      "$extensions": {
        "silvicom.section": [
          "The glow is the chip's OWN foot colour, read from the `to` stop rather than restated, so a re-stepped gradient cannot leave a cast from the colour it used to be. It carries no scheme of its own for the same reason — `--chip-*-to` already has one. Not exposed to Tailwind: it is an ingredient of the elevation below, never a utility."
        ]
      }
    },
